  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1247 AM MST Tue Nov 17 2026\n\nToday through Thu night...Slowly moving trough still has that\nsame cloud leaf sitting over southern Idaho. Precipitation has\nbeen slow to progress eastward. This will keep temperatures mild\nin eastern Idaho until the west side of the cloud leaf pushes\nto the east, which likely may not be until late tonight, at \nleast on the east side of the forecast area. Expect the heaviest\nrainfall this morning in the west, and in the afternoon in the\neast. Precipitation ends in the west early this evening, then\nlate tonight in the east. There should be a 12 to 18 hour break\nin precipitation, then the next shortwave digs farther south and\nentrains some subtropical moisture. However, not much gets\nbeyond the Sierra Nevada, at least by Thu, but it gets much\nwetter for Thu night. Thus this could actually produce more snow\nbecause it arrives at night. \n\nFri through Mon...Extensive cloud cover finally has\nan effect on afternoon highs by Fri with temperatures staying\nbelow 40 deg F. Sat/Sat Night appears to be a brief break before\nanother shortwave trough arrives at the west coast. This keeps\nSun and Mon wet, but slightly warmer as wind mostly has a west\nto southwest component, with some locations exceeding the 40 deg\nF threshold. \n\nWind will be blustery tonight, as long as the trough actually\npasses to the east. Fri night/Sat should also be windy with the\ntrough approach.",
